WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.340
Hi, I'm Scribble!

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.790
TabFM is Google's zero-shot model for spreadsheets.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.589
Here's how it works, read from its code.

00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:10.826
TabFM needs no column vocabulary.

00:00:10.826 --> 00:00:14.768
A column is just a position; all it knows about your schema

00:00:14.768 --> 00:00:16.740
is how many columns there are.

00:00:18.958 --> 00:00:21.737
A wrapper first turns the table into plain floats.

00:00:21.737 --> 00:00:25.441
Then each cell is grouped with the cells one and three columns

00:00:25.441 --> 00:00:26.985
to its right, and Fourier-expanded.

00:00:27.405 --> 00:00:30.636
Column attention and row attention alternate, twice each.

00:00:30.636 --> 00:00:33.867
Rows carry no order; columns get rotary positions.

00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:38.090
Eight CLS tokens per row are concatenated into one vector, two

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.473
thousand and forty-eight wide.

00:00:39.473 --> 00:00:41.547
The cell grid is thrown away.

00:00:41.968 --> 00:00:47.183
A twenty-four block transformer, ninety-eight point eight percent of the weights, reads those rows.

00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:50.536
Each row attends only to the labelled context rows.

00:00:50.956 --> 00:00:55.448
One catch: column order matters, so the default runs thirty-two passes, each

00:00:55.448 --> 00:00:57.693
with shuffled columns, and averages them.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:01.934
A gradient-boosted tree needs no shared input space.

00:01:01.934 --> 00:01:05.206
It re-derives the structure from your data, every time.

00:01:05.626 --> 00:01:10.046
TabFM trained on hundreds of millions of synthetic tables from causal models,

00:01:10.046 --> 00:01:12.625
so a new table's structure looks familiar.

00:01:13.925 --> 00:01:17.483
On the live board, TabFM sits three hundred and forty Elo above

00:01:17.483 --> 00:01:18.670
the best tuned tree.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.745
Four newer models now sit above it.

00:01:22.017 --> 00:01:26.308
Keep the table-reading front end tiny, and spend the depth on in-context

00:01:26.308 --> 00:01:27.738
learning over compressed rows.

00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:34.193
To recap: no column vocabulary, one vector per row, and a synthetic prior.

00:01:34.613 --> 00:01:36.742
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:36.742 --> 00:01:37.350
I'm Scribble.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:37.654
Bye!
